<v Daniel>How can the mathematical community possibly trust this? I mean, we are talking about an AI that operates without human intuition, outputting results that would take human experts months, if not years, to properly read, let alone verify.</v>

139
00:20:34.832 --> 00:20:49.576
<v Maya>This raises an important question about the future of peer review itself. If you read the README files on the Google GitHub repository, they promise that complete formalizations in Lean 4, which is a specialized computer-based interactive theorem prover,</v>

140
00:20:50.177 --> 00:20:51.857
<v Maya>are coming in a companion submission.</v>

141
00:20:52.317 --> 00:20:54.578
<v Daniel>But as of today, those Lean 4 files aren't there.</v>

142
00:20:54.936 --> 00:21:05.640
<v Maya>No, as of today, nobody outside of Google can run the code to check it. We are looking at a fundamental crisis of trust. How should mathematics treat unreviewed AI-generated proofs of this magnitude?</v>

143
00:21:05.888 --> 00:21:06.648
<v Daniel>It's unprecedented.</v>

144
00:21:06.988 --> 00:21:21.651
<v Maya>Exactly. And we are in the middle of a frantic AI math race right now. November 2026 has seen breathtaking claims from several different AI labs trying to outdo each other. The speed of progress is violently fast. What counts as</v>

145
00:21:21.711 --> 00:21:32.793
<v Maya>confirmation now? If an AI writes a 150 page proof that no single human can easily comprehend and we have to rely on another AI system like Lean 4 to tell us if it's correct, where does that leave us?</v>

146
00:21:33.175 --> 00:21:48.006
<v Daniel>It's dizzying. So what does this all mean for you listening? Let's summarize the landscape. We have just witnessed an incredible historic week for theoretical computer science. An AI agent independently broke major historical bounds,</v>

147
00:21:48.406 --> 00:21:57.633
<v Daniel>approved the LL1 third slider for factoring, achieved sub-quasi-polynomial bounds for discrete logs, and shattered deterministic records without needing the Riemann hypothesis.</v>

148
00:21:57.833 --> 00:21:59.074
<v Maya>Just an unbelievable week.</v>

149
00:21:59.314 --> 00:22:11.571
<v Daniel>But, crucially, there is absolutely no immediate threat to the encryption keeping your data safe today. The practical constants are too large, and the specific fields targeted aren't the ones your bank uses. The fortress holds.</v>

150
00:22:11.877 --> 00:22:26.603
<v Maya>The real takeaway isn't about encryption breaking today. It's about the philosophical shift and how scientific discovery happens. I want to leave you with this thought to ponder. If an AI can independently generate a 150-page proof that settles a</v>

151
00:22:26.863 --> 00:22:41.381
<v Maya>30-year-old math problem in just a few days, and humans now have to rely on other AI systems just to verify if it is true, What is the actual role of the human mathematician in the year 2027 and beyond? Are we</v>

152
00:22:41.421 --> 00:22:47.496
<v Maya>transitioning from being the creators and pioneers of mathematics to merely becoming the spectators of it?</v>

153
00:22:47.656 --> 00:22:56.819
<v Daniel>It's a wild thought. The mathematical fortress hasn't been breached by hackers, but the AI just showed us it can see the blueprint of the walls better than the architects who built them.</v>
